# Sprint 28a (1b, M13) — the aborts Linux is ALLOWED to have, by REASON.
#
# Each line is a substring; every abort the suite reports must match one of
# them. An abort matching nothing FAILS the job — that is the whole point: a
# new honest skip must be a decision somebody made, not a quiet loss of
# coverage.
#
# This is a pattern list, NOT a count, and the difference was measured. Two
# consecutive full-suite runs of the SAME work reported 2 aborts and then 4,
# because HotspotTest's profiling assumptions depend on the JFR sampler
# collecting enough samples, which depends on available CPU — and a parallel
# shard had just started spawning JVMs. Running HotspotTest alone: 11/11, zero
# aborts. A count-based budget would have failed that job for a reason with no
# defect behind it, and a gate that cries wolf gets ignored.
#
# Add a line only WITH the reason it is acceptable. Removing coverage is
# cheap; noticing that it went is what this file is for.

# The embedding corpus is a large artifact not carried in the repository; the
# tests that need it say so and skip. Supplying -Djawata.embed.corpus turns
# them back on.
no corpus at -Djawata.embed.corpus

# The profiler ranks SAMPLED measurements. Under contention the sampler can
# return too few samples to rank anything, and the test refuses to assert on a
# top row carrying one sample rather than report a coin toss as a result.
CPU profile too thin

# The matrix jobs deliberately set -Djawata.skip.gradle=true: they have no
# Gradle distribution, and without the flag the Tooling API DOWNLOADS one
# mid-suite, so those tests would pass or fail on whether services.gradle.org
# answers — three operating systems fetching ~150 MB on every push. The
# separate gradle-cell job is the environment built to prove these, and there
# a skip counts as a failure. Added after the first CI run of this gate: the
# budget had been derived from LOCAL runs, which do not set the flag.
Gradle Tooling API tests skipped via jawata.skip.gradle=true
